==============================================================================
W1  TIGHT BRANCH.  Parseval-normalised tight frame: F F^T = I_d  (columns = frame vectors)
    sum_n |<f,e_n>|^2 = f^T F F^T f = ||f||^2 exactly -- and after compression to any
    subspace P, the compressed frame is STILL Parseval.  Slack = 0 => no direction.
==============================================================================
  d=20, n_vectors=60, ||S-I||_inf = 4.441e-16  (tight, bound 1)
  rank P     max |sum_n|<Pf,Pe_n>|^2/k - ||Pf||^2| / ||Pf||^2
       1                                            6.570e-16
       4                                            9.908e-16
      10                                            6.770e-16
      16                                            7.273e-16
      20                                            1.206e-15
  -> worst Parseval defect over all compressions = 1.21e-15  (machine zero)
     P(sum|e_n><e_n|/k)P = P  is Cholesky-level.  The redistribution has NO slack:
     it is an identity in every subspace, hence it cannot produce a one-sided bound. (D17)

==============================================================================
W2  FRAME-NATIVE frame bounds: A = lam_min(S), B = lam_max(S).  S depends on the FRAME,
    not on the dynamics.  Vary L's gap from 2.0 to 0.0 (theory becomes gapless):
==============================================================================
  non-tight frame (1 basis + 6 vectors of a 2nd):  A = 1.000000   B = 2.000000   B/A = 2.0000
   gap Delta     A = lam_min(S)     B = lam_max(S)   moves?
        2.00       1.0000000000       2.0000000000       NO
        1.00       1.0000000000       2.0000000000       NO
        0.50       1.0000000000       2.0000000000       NO
        0.10       1.0000000000       2.0000000000       NO
        0.00       1.0000000000       2.0000000000       NO
  -> the frame bounds are constants of the frame.  They do not move when the gap closes,
     so they take the SAME value on the free massless field: acid test fails identically.
     A datum attached to the frame rather than to the theory cannot detect the theory. (D10)

  W2b  the only way to make A,B depend on L: build the frame FROM L.  The natural choice
       is the DIFFUSION frame  e_k = e^{-tL} delta_k  (k = 1..d).  Then
           S = sum_k e^{-tL}delta_k delta_k^T e^{-tL} = e^{-2tL},
       so   A = e^{-2t lam_max(L)}   and   B = e^{-2t*0} = 1.
       L = diag(0, Delta, 1.9, 3.1, 6.0),  lam_max = 6.0 (the UV end):
     t    Delta    A=e^{-2t*lam_max}          B  A moves with Delta?
  0.10     2.00         0.3011942119     1.0000                   NO
  0.10     1.00         0.3011942119     1.0000                   NO
  0.10     0.10         0.3011942119     1.0000                   NO
  0.10     0.00         0.3011942119     1.0000                   NO
  0.25     2.00         0.0497870684     1.0000                   NO
  0.25     1.00         0.0497870684     1.0000                   NO
  0.25     0.10         0.0497870684     1.0000                   NO
  0.25     0.00         0.0497870684     1.0000                   NO
     -> A is exactly the UV number e^{-2t*lam_max}; B is exactly the vacuum value 1.
        The gap -- the SECOND eigenvalue -- enters neither bound.  Ends, never the edge. (D15)
        NOTE: this A is the SAME UV quantity that the Choi analysis of candidate (2)
        produced as its 'positivity margin' (e^{-2t*lam_max}): two different candidate
        mechanisms hand back the identical UV number as their only positive reading.

